+"""Interlock zones for a multi-arm behaviour tree, derived from the plan's own trajectories (VASS style).
+
+    python -m agents.trajectory.tools.vass_zones --plan cells/<cell>/agentic_planner/plans/<tree>.json \
+        [--out plan_vass.json] [--zones zones.json] [--arbitration plan]
+
+The tree's arms coordinate by Parallel joins and WaitForRobot gates, and between
+those by the CLOCK: a Parallel step was checked on a shared timeline, so an arm
+that starts late or runs slow (a slow controller, an operator override, a lagging
+goal acceptance) meets its partner where the plan never checked. Measured on a
+U3 + fix-set-v3 plan of welding_cell_2_small: robot2 0.25 s late enters the cell's
+0.20 m warning band, 1.0 s late the 0.12 m arm spheres interpenetrate.
+
+The zones remove that dependence on timing:
+
+1. **Program paths.** Every arm's legs (ExecuteTrajectory, planned MoveHome) in
+   execution order, replayed on the executor's nominal clock; leg ``k`` of an arm is
+   its k-th leg in the walk. The joint polyline is densified so no envelope point
+   moves more than ``path_step_m`` between samples.
+2. **Conflicts.** Two samples of two arms conflict when a LOWER bound of their
+   distance is below a robot-robot rule of the cell plus ``margin_m``: umarell's own
+   inter-arm gate (the 0.12 m spheres of ``robot_kinematics._config_spheres`` against
+   ``collision.warning_distance``) and, when the cell declares
+   ``collision.hull_min_distance``, the URDF collision hulls (each hull enclosed by
+   the circumscribed spheres of its PCA box cut into near-cubic pieces). The bound
+   subtracts a step margin (sum_j |dq_j| R_j) so the continuous motion between
+   samples is covered. Sample times play no role: a conflict is purely geometric.
+3. **Zones.** Conflicting cells of each pair's coordination grid (blocks of samples)
+   are ordered by the nominal plan (who passes first), grouped into blobs, cut into
+   bands of ``band_s`` of the second arm's time and covered by rectangles that the
+   nominal path does not enter. A zone = two path intervals (one per arm) that are
+   never occupied at the same time; the PLC (``agents.cell.vass_plc``) enforces it.
+4. **Stop points** sit ``guard_s`` before each entry, moved earlier out of the
+   approach strokes and out of ``no_stop_radius_m`` around every weld: an arm never
+   waits with its electrode at a seam or inside the near-weld band.
+5. **Nominal check.** The interlocked plan is replayed at nominal speed; it must
+   complete (the certificate that plan-order arbitration cannot deadlock under any
+   timing) and it reports the nominal waits.
+
+6. **Lockstep** (``ZoneConfig.lockstep``: umarell's opt-in LOCKSTEP=1, robots.yaml
+   ``vass: {lockstep: ...}`` or ``$LOCKSTEP``). The legs of one ompl-compound step
+   (``sync_group``) start off one barrier and the executor e-stops on drift, so no
+   zone is derived between two arms' legs of one group (the compound plan proves
+   them apart at each instant; the rest at the leg before a group counts as the
+   group's start, and zone rectangles are cut at group boundaries), the nominal
+   clock and the nominal check have the barrier, and every zone a lockstep leg
+   enters stops at that leg's START (granted at rest, never in motion).
+
+The result is ``behaviour_tree.metadata.vass`` (the ``vass_zones_v1`` table) plus an
+``interlocks`` port on every leg that overlaps a zone. Executors without a PLC
+ignore the port, so the annotated tree runs exactly as before.
+"""
+from __future__ import annotations
+
+import argparse
+import json
+import math
+import sys
+import time
+from dataclasses import asdict, dataclass, field
+from typing import Optional
+
+import numpy as np
+
+from agents.cell import vass_protocol as P
+from agents.trajectory.tools.btcpp import (
+    children as bt_children, node_kind, port as bt_port, resolve as bt_resolve, to_xml, trajectory_of,
+)
